2 Related Works
Single-Cell LLMs. Single-cell sequencing technologies provide diverse biological features that facilitate the interpretation of cellular structures and functions [22, 23]. Early efforts in scRNAseq analysis focused on statistical approaches such as Seurat [24] and Harmony [25]. Advances in scRNA-seq have also generated massive, high-precision transcriptomic datasets, driving the development of Single-Cell LLMs (scLLMs) [26]. This technique quantifies the mRNA molecule abundance, producing gene expression matrices that record expression values of individual genes across cells [27]. Previous works have developed transformer-based foundation models on scRNA-seq data, pre-training with masked learning objectives on millions of cells [28, 8, 10, 11]. Subsequent works improve the learning process by incorporating cell labels, such as batch information [9] and other cell metadata [14]. After fine-tuning, these LLMs have proven useful in practical downstream tasks such as cell-type annotation, cell clustering, and batch effect removal.
Cell-Text Modeling. Incorporating free-text biological descriptions has proven useful for improving cellular representation learning, with prior works demonstrating that such auxiliary textual supervision can enhance the quality of cell embeddings [16, 13, 29, 12]. Beyond representation learning, enabling bidirectional translation between cells and text facilitates tasks such as universal cell-type annotation [30] and the generation of pseudo-cells [31, 32]. Recent efforts aim to build multimodal LLMs for single-cell data that align cell and text modalities directly [15, 19]. After instruction tuning on cell annotation or pseudo-cell generation, these models enable interactive single-cell analyses for human users. Nevertheless, the most crucial single-cell analysis tasks that boost real-world scientific discoveries rely more on efficient cell representation extraction to support tasks like cell annotation and clustering, which is often neglected in previous multimodal approaches.
Scientific Multimodal LLMs. Multimodal LLMs show remarkable potential for integrating data from various modalities [33-35], inspiring research for scientific modalities. Existing works have constructed multimodal LLMs for small molecules [36-38] and proteins [39, 40] to tackle crossmodal scientific problems, such as description generation, molecular property prediction, and textconditioned de novo design [41-46]. Although single-cell analysis holds comparable scientific importance, the sparsity and high dimensionality nature of scRNA-seq data introduce a significant gap between transcriptomic and textual modalities, presenting challenges for joint modeling of them.

--- CHUNK 10 ---
2 Related Works
Figure 3: Overview of scMMGPT. (1) Cross-modal Discriminative Objective: Given paired cell and text inputs, the model learns to identify the correct textual description of a cell by aligning the outputs of the scLLM and text LLM. (2, 3) Cross-modal Generative Objectives: scMMGPT strengthens multimodal alignment through a unified generative pre-training strategy, jointly optimizing cell-to-text and text-to-cell translation tasks to facilitate bidirectional knowledge transfer.

SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE

Figure type: schematic workflow/model architecture diagram for multimodal cell information modeling.

Visible panels/labels: left panel titled “Multimodal Cell Information” shows biological evidence in `scRNA-seq Matrices` and domain knowledge in `Textual Descriptions`; center shows “Gene Expression Levels,” “Gene Tokens,” and “Text Token Sequence”; right shows an `scLLM` and `Text LLM` connected by cross-modal projectors, with three downstream tasks: `Cell-Text Discrimination`, `Text-to-Cell Generation`, and `Cell-to-Text Generation`.

Quantity shown: gene expression levels, discretized gene tokens, and textual cell descriptions used as multimodal inputs.

Main visible finding/message: the figure presents a model framework that aligns single-cell RNA-seq representations with text descriptions, enabling relevance scoring and bidirectional generation between cell profiles and textual descriptions.

--- CHUNK 11 ---
3 Method
To build scMMGPT, we first construct a diverse cell-text dataset to support cross-modal training (§3.1). scMMGPT adopts a specialized scLLM that directly models original expression levels to enable robust cell representation learning and bypass information loss, alongside a pre-trained text LLM for generating descriptive annotations (§3.2). To facilitate comprehensive cell-text alignment, we introduce bidirectional projectors and a two-stage pre-training strategy combining discriminative with generative objectives (§3.3). After pre-training, scMMGPT can be applied to a range of downstream single-cell analysis tasks (§3.4). Figure 3 illustrates the overall architecture of scMMGPT.

--- CHUNK 12 ---
3 Method
3.1 Large Scale and Multi-Source Cell-Text Data Collection
Large-Scale Single-Cell Transcriptomics Collection. For single-cell transcriptomics data, we collect 60 million single-cell profiles from the biggest single-cell transcriptomics database CellxGene [20]. The collected cellular data includes high-resolution scNRA-seq matrices with gene names and numeric expression levels and associated cell properties such as cell types, tissues, and disease statuses. To ensure data quality, we then conduct data filtering and deduplication (see Appendix B for more details). After these steps, we maintain scRNA-seq data of 27 million cells across various human tissues, summarized in Figure 4. The diverse cell atlas ensures generalization and prevents scMMGPT from degenerating into overfitting specific tissues.
Large-Scale Cell Description Collection. For textual information, we gather free-form cell identity explanation ( e.g., , definition of cell types and diseases) from two sources: (1) the OBO Foundry [21], which integrates professional reference for biomedical terms, and (2) Wikipedia, which contains comprehensible explanations of cell function. We construct textual descriptions of cells by merging the free-form cell explanations and the cell metadata from CellxGenes.
